{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\ni=json.load(open('draft/tmp/interp_transfer.json'))\nprint('RATIONALE len',len(i['rationale']))\nprint('COUNTER',len(i['counterevidence']))\nfor c in i['counterevidence']: print('---'); print(c)\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\ni=json.load(open('draft/tmp/interp_transfer.json'))\nprint('RATIONALE len',len(i['rationale']))\nprint('COUNTER',len(i['counterevidence']))\nfor c in i['counterevidence']: print('---'); print(c)\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"RATIONALE len 10060\nCOUNTER 8\n---\nThe documentation's promise is soft rather than a guarantee: it says it is 'very likely' the target wins and that a follower which cannot be promoted 'will fail gracefully' (api.go:1296-1330), and the leaderLoop code deliberately reports nil with the message 'lost leadership during transfer (expected)', which can be read as the future reporting only that leadership was relinquished. The claim therefore rests on the admission of an ineligible target and on the observed outcome, not on the future's wording alone.\n---\nThe control cases show the same mechanism transferring leadership correctly: the explicit transfer to an ordinary voter installed that named voter with a single term advance, and the later automatic control completed in about a hundred milliseconds with leadership on a voter and no leaderless samples. The defect is therefore specific to the eligibility of an explicitly named target, not to leadership transfer in general or to the entry-point harness's sequencing.\n---\nThe measured disruption is small in absolute terms because the cluster ran with inmemConfig timeouts (Heartbeat/Election/Lease 50 ms): the three trigger runs recorded 8, 7 and 6 leaderless samples (about 80, 70 and 60 ms) and one extra election round, against zero leaderless samples in the voter controls. The interval follows the configured election timeout, so a production configuration of about 1 s would scale it accordingly; these millisecond figures are not a bound for other settings.\n---\nNo committed entry was lost, no vote was counted for an ineligible candidate, and no decision-safety property was contradicted in either case; what is contradicted is the completion signal and the authority outcome of the explicit transfer.\n---\nBoth corrected runs show the target's own configuration had already converged to non-voter before the request, so the exercised case is a member that knew it could not win; a demoted member whose own view still lagged (a stale configuration) was not exercised, although the earliest runs exercised an unknown mix.\n---\nThe demotion race is a negative result for elevation: in 20 attempts issued back to back, no target became leader and every transfer reported success, so the timing gap that could have produced a worse outcome did not do so in this harness.\n---\nThe future's result is not uniform across receipts: the demotion race receipt 62e8b8bdcc9f448687b6588dda84125d recorded successful transfer futures in all twenty attempts, while receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b recorded 'leadership transfer in progress' on every one of five attempts on a four-member cluster whose demoted member's view had already converged. The sentence that the future completes without error therefore describes those receipts and not the operation in general, and the durable observation common to both is that the named member is never installed and no suffrage check refuses it. The repeat of the entry-point comparison, receipt e023eecf02c74f4db5bc8c5a16fd3586, reproduced the in-progress surface exactly, with the same error on all five attempts over 1036 ms, so within that harness the surface is stable. A second run of the demotion race, receipt 334d7a88f3b244548f316bcdb42b779e, reproduced that surface, with twenty successful futures and no attempt in which the named member led.\n---\nThe in-progress error seen in receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b is not reproduced here: with the transfer flag clear before the call, the same explicit request returned no error at all. The account therefore cannot claim that the error is a stable consequence of naming a non-voter, only that neither outcome is a refusal keyed to the target's suffrage and neither installs the target.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\ni=json.load(open('draft/tmp/interp_transfer.json'))\nfor c in i['limitations']: print('---'); print(c)\nprint('=== JOINT'); print(i['joint_history'])\nprint('=== RATIONALE TAIL'); print(i['rationale'][-2200:])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\ni=json.load(open('draft/tmp/interp_transfer.json'))\nfor c in i['limitations']: print('---'); print(c)\nprint('=== JOINT'); print(i['joint_history'])\nprint('=== RATIONALE TAIL'); print(i['rationale'][-2200:])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"---\nThe cluster size is four members throughout, with three voters after the demotion, and the transfer always targets a non-leader member. The demotion race covers a transfer issued concurrently with the demotion, but a cluster whose only non-voter is the sole reachable member and a transfer that lands while the leader is already losing office to another election were not tested.\n---\nLeaderless counts are sampled every ~10 ms over roughly 4.7 s of the 5 s window (470 to 471 samples); a gap shorter than the sample spacing would be missed, so 60 to 80 ms is a lower bound for the trigger and the controls' zero does not prove uninterrupted leadership.\n---\nOnly immediate convergence was observed in the corrected runs: the demoted member's own view reported the new role within the first poll (0 ms), so no single-case run exercised a target whose configuration still showed it as a voter at request time. The demotion race issues the transfer before applying, but it records the target's final role rather than its view at request time.\n---\nThat the voter members had committed the demotion before the transfer is inferred from their rejection and term adoption, not from reading each voter's configuration.\n---\nOut of scope: whether an unprivileged or misconfigured peer could send TimeoutNow directly, even though timeoutNow performs no term, leader or membership check (raft.go:2232-2237); that path was not exercised here.\n---\nThree harnesses now give two reproduced and one single futures outcome for the same request: the demotion race returned successful futures in all twenty attempts on both of its runs, the entry-point harness returned 'leadership transfer in progress' on all five attempts on both of its runs, and the flag probe returned no error on its single run with the transfer flag clear. The difference between the flag probe and the other two therefore lies in their preceding steps, which include repeated leader and configuration polling before the transfer, and which of those steps sets the transfer state is not yet identified; all three keep the demoted member's own view reporting the non-voter role before the call.\n=== JOINT\nAll five receipts belong to this investigation. Receipts d436029b08c5425aa5aec577901cb8ba, 0726bba116ac415fa38b99b96a88290f and 4185f1615cf54e4fbd4801044480416d are successive revisions of the same fixed experiment: the first run, a revision adding per-member state sampling, and the revision that reads the target's own configuration after the demotion. Receipt 3324fe87d6da49c6b53eab0e116823df is a clean-copy repeat of the third revision executed from its retained inputs, with identical case names, admissions and assertions. Receipt 62e8b8bdcc9f448687b6588dda84125d comes from a separate experiment identifier, cf5136d55dd74f22ad66c73229d55f35-experiment, which keeps the same demote-then-transfer sequence but starts both requests back to back on a fresh cluster per attempt and summarises twenty attempts; it intervenes on the same mechanism under the same question, and its outcome is compatible with the single-case receipts. Receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b comes from a further experiment identifier, 6f0e1da8cb8048f5b28060a73243fa6b-experiment, which keeps the same demote-then-transfer shape and adds an automatic-transfer control on the same four-member cluster shape plus a client write after the transfer attempt. Receipt 78769a572c5b4b249e441ac7ce63422a comes from a further experiment identifier, 2690c8dadb9c4631b3fe90d9626cadb9-experiment, which keeps the demote-then-transfer setup and adds sampling of the leader's transfer flag around the call, so the earlier receipt's in-progress error can be attributed rather than assumed. Receipt e023eecf02c74f4db5bc8c5a16fd3586 is a repeat execution of 6f0e1da8cb8048f5b28060a73243fa6b-experiment from its retained inputs, so its cases, admissions and assertions match receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b. Receipt 334d7a88f3b244548f316bcdb42b779e is a repeat execution of cf5136d55dd74f22ad66c73229d55f35-experiment from its retained inputs, so its case, admissions and assertions match receipt 62e8b8bdcc9f448687b6588dda84125d.\n=== RATIONALE TAIL\nogress error is not evidence of a pre-existing transfer; and because the future succeeds while the named member never leads, the surface the caller sees is success, not a refusal, which is the form of the gap the account claims.\n\nRepeat of the entry-point comparison, receipt e023eecf02c74f4db5bc8c5a16fd3586 (completed, tests_passed, exit 0): the same fixed inputs were run again with no changes. Parsed index 0 records the control's state and parsed index 1 its result: one automatic attempt completed in 106 ms with no error, leadership ended on a voter that is not the demoted member, no leaderless sample was seen in 26 samples, and a client Apply and GetConfiguration afterwards both succeeded in about 2 ms. Parsed index 2 records the trigger's state and parsed index 3 its result: the first attempt already returned 'leadership transfer in progress', all five attempts over 1036 ms returned the same error, the named member never reported Leader, the final leader is a different voter, and the post-transfer Apply and GetConfiguration both succeeded. The in-progress surface therefore reproduces exactly in this harness across two runs, with the same error, the same five attempts and the same roughly one-second span, so it is a stable property of this sequence rather than a one-off event; the flag probe's clean first call remains a different sequence, and the difference between them is not yet identified.\n\nRepeat of the demotion race, receipt 334d7a88f3b244548f316bcdb42b779e (completed, tests_passed, exit 0): the same randomised loop of twenty attempts ran again. Parsed index 0 records the case with four members and twenty attempts, and parsed index 1 its result: twenty demotions succeeded, twenty transfer futures completed with an empty last error, the named member led in none of the attempts, no attempt ended with it leading, and it ended as a non-voter in all twenty. The nil-future surface is therefore reproducible in this harness across two runs, alongside the entry-point harness's reproducible in-progress surface and the flag probe's single clean call, so the account now holds two independently reproduced futures outcomes for the same request rather than one of each.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
